earch3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Analysis Paralysis, oder abgekürzt AP, ist ein Problem, das immer wieder in Brettspielrunden auftaucht. ... Brettspiele sollen ein schönes Hobby sein, das Spaß macht und zwar allen Personen am Tisch.\n\n# Was ist Analysis Paralysis? Wie geht man mit diesen Spielern am besten um?\n\n19. Februar 2024 / Peer / 2 Kommentare\n\nImage\n\nAnalysis Paralysis, oder abgekürzt AP, ist ein Problem, das immer wieder in Brettspielrunden auftaucht.\n\nWas das genau ist, wie es sich negativ auf den Spieleabend auswirken kann und was man dagegen tun kann, erfahrt ihr heute.\n\nIch freue mich sehr auf eure Erfahrungsberichte zu Analysis Paralysis und über eure Tipps, wie ihr damit umgeht.\n\nFür evtl. enthaltene Werbe-Links auf dieser Seite zahlt der Händler ggf. eine Provision. Diese Werbe-Links sind am Sternchen (*) zu erkennen. Für dich ändert sich nichts am Preis. Mehr Infos.\n\n## Was ist Analysis Paralysis?\n\nMan könnte Analysis Paralysis mit Paralyse durch Analyse oder auch Entscheidungslähmung übersetzen laut Wikipedia.\n\nDabei handelt es sich um ein Phänomen, das nicht nur in Brettspielen auftauchen kann. Es betrifft Menschen, die sich nicht zu einer Entscheidung durchringen können, da sie die Situation übermäßig lang analysieren.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLittle List Is The Simplest GTD App Ever Made - MacStories (https://www.macstories.net/reviews/little-list-is-the-simplest-gtd-app-ever-made/)\nciteturn27search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; …Or maybe it’s not really a GTD app at all. ... Federico is also the co-host of AppStories, a weekly podcast exploring the world of apps, Unwind, a fun exploration of media and more, and NPC: Next Portable Console, a show about portable gaming and the handheld revolution.\n\n# Little List Is The Simplest GTD App Ever Made\n\nBy Federico Viticci\n\n…Or maybe it’s not really a GTD app at all. Little List is an iPhone app developed by Caleb Thorson, the same guy behind the Trickle Twitter client we reviewed here. And just like Trickle, Little List is a minimal, elegant and focused app that takes a simple approach at a complex system: getting things done. Instead of providing tags, folders, projects and contexts, Little List is, well, a simple and clean list of things you have to do.\n\nMany apps in the App Store have tried to go extremely simple against the most fundamental GTD principles. Little List, however, has a cool trick up its sleeve: it’s got yet another implementation of Loren Brichter’s “Pull to refresh”, but instead of refreshing, the gesture sorts items. The command is, in fact, called “Pull to sort”. So what do you sort? Normal items and starred ones. You can create a new entry by tapping on the + button and start typing; if you have important tasks you’d like to highlight, you can star them with an additional tap. “Pull to sort” will put the starred items on top.\n\nLittle List is as simple as it gets. It’s available at $0.99, it doesn’t have any kind of OTA sync – it doesn’t have any kind of anything, actually. It’s just a list, with starred items. And with a nice icon. Give it a try if you’re looking for a different take on aggregating your to dos.\n\nTags: iPhone\n\nFederico Viticci\n\nFederico is the founder and Editor-in-Chief of MacStories, where he writes about Apple with a focus on apps, developers, iPad, and iOS productivity. He founded MacStories in April 2009 and has been writing about Apple since. Federico is also the co-host of AppStories, a weekly podcast exploring the world of apps, Unwind, a fun exploration of media and more, and NPC: Next Portable Console, a show about portable gaming and the handheld revolution.\n\n@[email protected]\n\n@viticci.macstories.net--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nreviews Archives - MacStories (https://www.macstories.net/reviews/)\nciteturn27search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Plus, Structured got a Liquid Glass update, which makes the app feel right at home among other modern apps without sacrificing the app’s simple design and legibility. ... With the ability to start a new note or append to existing notes, you can get quite a lot done with only your voice. ... It’s an excellent app and scraps of paper is no way to live.\n# Our Favorite Indie Apps for iOS 27, Vol. 3\n\nBy John Voorhees\n\nImage\n\nWe have another collection our favorite indie apps that take advantage of Apple’s latest OS features. Today, we’re highlighting: Haven, Structured, Capture, Scrappy, and Fin.\n\nImage\n\n### Haven\n\nHaven is a HomeKit and Matter pairing code utility by João Franco for the iPhone, iPad, Mac, and Apple Watch that added Siri AI features this fall. The app is exactly what you want with this sort of utility because it makes storing codes for safekeeping fast and easy. Later when you need to re-pair a smart plug behind a couch or a lightbulb in a hard-to-reach spot, you’ll be glad you dropped the codes into Haven.\n\nRetrieving codes is made even simpler, thanks to Haven’s Siri AI integration. Just ask for a code based on the name of your device in Haven, and Siri will fetch the code for you without opening the app. The feature works with both HomeKit and Matter codes and is compatible with natural language queries. # Our Favorite Indie Apps for iOS 27, Vol. 2\n\nBy John Voorhees\n\nImage\n\nIt’s time to get back to our favorite indie apps that take advantage of Apple’s latest OS features. Today, we’re highlighting: Bear, Gentler Streak, The Outsiders, DoMarks, and Cheatsheet Notes.\n\nJohn\n\nImage\n\n### Bear\n\nBear, the note-taking app for iPhone, iPad, Mac, and Apple Watch, just released version 2.10 with several Siri AI features, including actions to:\n\n  * Create a new note\n  * Append text to an existing note\n  * Append files to a note\n  * Pin notes\n\nImage\n\nThe app also includes onscreen awareness so Siri knows the content of the note you’re looking at without you telling it.\n\nBear’s new actions make it a compelling way to dictate notes on the go. Simply invoke Siri and start talking. With the ability to start a new note or append to existing notes, you can get quite a lot done with only your voice. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nADHD paralysis: why you get stuck and how to start tasks · Focus Tools (https://focus.tools/articles/adhd-paralysis-how-to-start/)\nciteturn27search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 2 days ago; # ADHD paralysis: why you get stuck and how to start tasks\n\nADHD\n\n# ADHD paralysis: why you get stuck and how to start tasks\n\nWhat ADHD paralysis is, why starting a task can feel impossible, and six small steps that help you get moving when you're frozen at the desk.\n\nBy the Focus Tools editors · October 12, 2026 · 7 min read\n\nYour laptop is open and the cursor is blinking in an empty email draft. You know what to say. Your afternoon depends on sending it. Forty-five minutes later you're still staring at the screen, your chest is tight, and your brain is busy planning how to reorganize the bookshelf.\n\nFrom the doorway, you look lazy. From the inside, it feels like pushing on a door that won't move.\n\nPeople call this ADHD paralysis. It isn't a formal diagnosis, but it names something real: the gap between knowing what you want to do and actually starting.\n\nShort answer\n\nADHD paralysis is an informal term for freezing before a task you want or need to do.\n\nIt's usually linked to executive dysfunction, mismatched stimulation, or emotional overload, not laziness.\n\nThe most reliable fix is to shrink the first step to under two minutes, write it down, and add some outside structure.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTime Review: Powered by AI and You - MacStories (https://www.macstories.net/reviews/time-review-powered-by-ai-and-you/)\nciteturn27search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Time Review: Powered by AI and You ... The story of Time is one that you’d expect to hear on stage at WWDC: two teenage developers, Shaan Singh (17) and Erik van Der Plas (15), create an app to help you be more productive. ... I love having the ability to dump small tasks and work on them individually, watching my progress continue as the timer counts down.\n\nAt the very bottom of the task list is the total number of tasks and time it’ll take to finish your work, along with an updating clock that will show you the estimated time of completing all your tasks.\n\n## AI Implementation\n\nTo help you work better, Time’s artificial intelligence learns your work habits and offers suggestions based on the time of day and the length of the task. For example, Time may encourage you to extend the time limit on a certain task that you’re not productive on or work on something at a different time of the day.\n\nIn my usage of Time, I haven’t seen this option displayed on any of my tasks. I’m not quite sure how long it takes to display, but I imagine that the app takes quite a bit of time before it knows your habits enough to suggest alternatives.\n\nFor your more repetitive tasks, Time will offer autocomplete suggestions based on what you’ve typed before.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nFor my smaller, more immediate tasks, Time is looking like my next go-to productivity tool. I love having the ability to dump small tasks and work on them individually, watching my progress continue as the timer counts down. Through using Time, I’ve seen myself become more motivated and encouraged to get stuff done.\n\nFor a 1.0 product, Time nails its goals and looks good while doing it. While it isn’t for the task manager power users, those looking for motivational new ways to get tasks completed should look into Time.\n\nYou can pick up Time for $0.99 on the App Store for iPhone.\n\nJake Underwood\n\nFormer MacStories 